<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-chapter-7" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Triple Integrals and Coordinate Systems</title>

<introduction>
  <p>
    In this chapter, we extend integration into three dimensions.
    Triple integrals allow us to compute quantities such as volume, mass, and average value over
    solid regions in space. As with double integrals, success depends not only on computation but
    also on understanding the geometry of the region being integrated over.
  </p>

  <p>
    We begin by evaluating triple integrals over rectangular boxes and general bounded regions.
    Changing the order of integration plays an important role, and careful attention to bounds helps
    ensure correct setup. These ideas reinforce the connection between algebraic limits of integration
    and the geometric shape of a region.
  </p>

  <p>
    The chapter then introduces cylindrical and spherical coordinate systems.
    These coordinate choices align naturally with common three-dimensional geometries and often
    simplify both region descriptions and calculations. By the end of the chapter, you should be able
    to select an appropriate coordinate system and efficiently compute triple integrals for a wide
    variety of solid regions.
  </p>
</introduction>

